From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: typec: ucsi: glink: do not send to a protection domain that is down

Every audio DSP stop reports a driver error for something that is working
exactly as designed:

    # echo stop > /sys/class/remoteproc/remoteproc0/state
    ucsi_glink.pmic_glink_ucsi pmic_glink.ucsi.0: failed to send UCSI write request: -5

The UCSI interface lives in a protection domain on the ADSP. PDR announces
that domain going down, pmic_glink_ucsi_pdr_notify() records it in pd_running
and hands the rest to pmic_glink_ucsi_register(), which calls
ucsi_unregister() -- and ucsi_unregister() disables notifications as its very
first act, before it has torn anything down:

    /* Disable notifications */
    ucsi->ops->async_control(ucsi, UCSI_SET_NOTIFICATION_ENABLE);

That write goes out over a link whose remote has just gone away, so
rpmsg_send() fails and the driver reports it. There is nothing to fix on the
far side and nothing the caller can do with the error -- ucsi_unregister()
does not even look at it.

So answer from what PDR already told us rather than finding out from the
transport: with the PD down, fail reads and writes immediately with
-ECONNRESET, which is what pmic_glink_send() returns once the endpoint itself
is gone. Same outcome, no error reported for an expected condition.

Note this also silences a genuine send failure while the PD is down, which is
the intent: during that window every send fails, and a driver that was told
the service is gone should not be talking to it.

Not hibernation-specific: a plain SSR reproduces it, and the hibernate path
merely performs a stop/start.

---
--- a/drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi_glink.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi_glink.c
@@ -81,6 +81,31 @@
 	u8 read_buf[UCSI_BUF_V2_SIZE];
 };
 
+/*
+ * The interface this driver talks to lives in a protection domain on the
+ * ADSP, and PDR tells us when that domain goes away. Anything sent after
+ * that point cannot be answered, and one such send is unavoidable:
+ * ucsi_unregister() disables notifications as its first act, so taking the
+ * PD down -- an SSR, or the DSP stop/start a hibernation cycle performs --
+ * always ended in
+ *
+ *   failed to send UCSI write request: -5
+ *
+ * reported as a driver error although nothing is wrong. Answer from what PDR
+ * already told us instead of finding out from the transport.
+ */
+static bool pmic_glink_ucsi_pd_running(struct pmic_glink_ucsi *ucsi)
+{
+	unsigned long flags;
+	bool running;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&ucsi->state_lock, flags);
+	running = ucsi->pd_running;
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&ucsi->state_lock, flags);
+
+	return running;
+}
+
 static int pmic_glink_ucsi_read(struct ucsi *__ucsi, unsigned int offset,
 				void *val, size_t val_len)
 {
@@ -94,6 +119,12 @@
 	req.hdr.opcode = UC_UCSI_READ_BUF_REQ;
 
 	mutex_lock(&ucsi->lock);
+
+	if (!pmic_glink_ucsi_pd_running(ucsi)) {
+		ret = -ECONNRESET;
+		goto out_unlock;
+	}
+
 	memset(ucsi->read_buf, 0, sizeof(ucsi->read_buf));
 	reinit_completion(&ucsi->read_ack);
 
@@ -143,6 +174,9 @@
 	int ret;
 	u8 *buf;
 
+	if (!pmic_glink_ucsi_pd_running(ucsi))
+		return -ECONNRESET;
+
 	req.hdr.owner = PMIC_GLINK_OWNER_USBC;
 	req.hdr.type = MSG_TYPE_REQ_RESP;
 	req.hdr.opcode = UC_UCSI_WRITE_BUF_REQ;
